<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feidlimid mac Crimthainn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/847">847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feidlimid mac Crimthainn was king of Munster from 820 to 847 and also a churchman, counted as an abbot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. He raided great monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> and challenged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, pressing a claim to be high king of Ireland. The Irish annals record both his violence against churches and his reputation for learning and piety.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Feidlimid belonged to the Cenél Fíngin sept of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganacht-chaisil">Eóganacht Chaisil</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a> and took the kingship of Munster in 820. He was counted among the Céli Dé and as abbot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonfert">Clonfert</a>. In 823, together with Bishop Artrí mac Conchobar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>, he had the Law of St. Patrick established in Munster and sacked the monastery of Gailline of the Britons in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-offaly">County Offaly</a>. In 827 he held the first of several royal meetings with Conchobar mac Donnchada of the Southern Uí Néill, King of Tara.</p><p>His campaigns struck at kingdoms and churches alike. In 830 he probably burned Fore Abbey and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-briuin">Uí Briúin</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a>, and in 831 and 832 he led an army of Leinster and Munster into East Meath as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slane">Slane</a> and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a>. In 833 he burned it again, together with the Clann Cholmáin monastery of Durrow. In 836 he took the oratory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> against Forindam, abbot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>, and imprisoned him with the congregation of Patrick, and in 837 he took the abbacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>.</p><p>After a great royal meeting in 838 with Niall Caille mac Áeda, King of the Northern Uí Néill, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-inisfallen">Annals of Inisfallen</a>, which probably follow Munster tradition, report that Feidlimid became full king of Ireland that day and occupied the abbot's chair of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonfert">Clonfert</a>. In 840, probably the height of his power, he ravaged Mide and Brega and rested at Tara, where he seized Gormlaith, daughter of Murchad, king of Laigin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 841 Niall Caille defeated him at Magh-Ochtar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a>, apparently by surprise; a verse tells how Feidlimid's crozier was abandoned in the blackthorns and taken by Niall by right of victory. He seems never to have recovered and died in 847.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> give no cause of death, but most other sources blame St. Kieran, patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a>, avenging the plunder of his monastery. The Annals of Clonmacnoise tell that the saint appeared to the king in his bed and struck him in the belly with his staff, bringing on the illness that killed him. The same annals add that, for all his love of plunder, some numbered him among the scribes and anchorites of Ireland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fedelmid mac Crimthainn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Several were also churchmen or patrons of learning, such as <b>Feidlimid mac Crimthainn</b>, king and abbot, who died in 847.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Kingdom of Munster</a></li></ul></section>
